Original commit body from ac5d010e01fb3632ac5dbf595c0db929be38407d:

Every per-language return-type registry in the tree is built from ANALYSED
DECLARATIONS: an analyzer fills `FileAnalysis.method_return_types` in Pass 1 and
the aggregation hands it to Pass 2. A receiver bound to a LIBRARY call therefore
cannot be typed at all — there is no declaration in the repository to register —
and the 820 method-kind `io_primitives` rows across nine languages that need a
typed receiver are unreachable however correct they are.

WHY A SEPARATE FAMILY, not a `returns:` key on the io rows (WI-lalot's own
ordering): it is a SIGNATURE fact, not an I/O fact, and one file whose declared
job is "what boundary does this cross" should not also answer "what type does
this return" — the shape INV-tutar cost 134 misclassified rows on; the CONSUMER
is `var_types` in nine analyzers, which also feeds slice quality, centrality,
dead code and taint; and one table shape serves nine languages where an
io_primitives extension would be nine parallel additions able to drift.

THE MERGE HAPPENS IN FOUR PLACES, NOT ONE. I wrote "one line in base.py" before
checking, and the code says otherwise: `analyze_go` and `analyze_objc` are
standalone functions with their own aggregations, and `java.py` builds its
registry from `Symbol.meta["return_type"]` rather than from
`FileAnalysis.method_return_types` at all. A name-based grep found three of the
four — `objc.py` calls its variable `_method_return_types` — so the enumeration
is by what the code DOES (`.method_return_types.items()`), not by variable name.
All four merge with `setdefault` AFTER the analysed rows, so a declaration in the
repository always beats a catalogue guess.

GO ADDITIONALLY NEEDED A LOOKUP, not just a merge. Its registry is consulted for
a BARE call (`makeConn()`) and for a method on a TYPED variable, but a
package-qualified producer — `net.Listen(...)`, `exec.Command(...)` — takes the
selector branch, where `net` is a package rather than a variable, so
`func_vars.get("net")` is empty and the lookup never happens. The merge alone
left `ln.Accept()` and `c.Start()` on the `external` sentinel, which is exactly
the inertness this item was filed for. The registry itself is the gate for the
new branch: an analysed go key is a bare function name or `Receiver.Method` with
a conventionally capitalised Go type, so a hit on a lowercase `package.Func` key
can only come from a library row.

PYTHON'S SECOND HOME IS REMOVED RATHER THAN JOINED. `py.py`'s
`TYPE_PRESERVING_MEMBERS` enumerated ten `pathlib.Path` members inline under a
comment stating that the concept's home is the return-type registry and that the
table existed only because "the types here ship no source for Pass 1 to parse".
That catalogue now exists, so the table is DERIVED from it — a member whose
declared return type IS its owner preserves the type — and the propagation rule
in `_preserved_receiver_type` is untouched, because widening which receivers
carry a type must not widen which members preserve it.

THE USER CHANNEL IS READ, NOT MERELY DECLARED. The first version registered
`library_signatures.d` in the registry and taught no loader to look at it — which
is exactly the defect ADR-0047 ruling 3 was written about, where `io_primitives.d`
was advertised to users while nothing scanned it. The registry's own gates caught
it: `FAMILY_CONSEQUENCE` refuses an unregistered family and
`test_declared_extensible_is_not_reported_as_readable` pins wired-vs-declared so a
channel cannot silently claim to be usable. The loader now reads the channel
through the shared `user_channel_files` helper whose docstring already anticipated
a fourth caller, with a user row REPLACING a shipped one — the shipped catalogue
is stdlib-only, so a collision means the user knows something about their own
build that this file cannot.

MEASURED, BOTH ARMS COLD, AND THE ITEM'S HEADLINE CLAIM DOES NOT SURVIVE IT.
io-boundaries are IDENTICAL between arms on all five repositories:

  caddy 299, gocryptfs 253, guacamole-client 93, AFNetworking 15, Alamofire 40

Traced on a minimal fixture: `ln, _ := net.Listen(...); ln.Accept()` already
produces a `net_recv` chain with NO library row, even though the analyzer emits
`go:external:0-0:Accept`. Something downstream already recovers it, so the
premise that these rows unlock otherwise-unmatchable catalogue rows is false for
the stdlib rows shipped here. (A first A/B appeared to show the opposite; that
run reused a cached behavior map because `io-boundaries` does not re-run the
analyzer. Re-run cold, the arms are equal.)

WHAT THE ROWS DO CHANGE is the analyzer's module slot, and every moved row was
read back:

  caddy      7,271 -> 7,314 typed slots;  43 sites external -> typed;  0 regressions
  gocryptfs  3,734 -> 4,009 typed slots; 274 sites external -> typed;  0 regressions
             (its `external` bucket drops 704 -> 429, a 39% reduction)

    191  os        file.Truncate(0)                <- os.Create / os.OpenFile
     81  os/exec   out, err = cmd.CombinedOutput()
     19  net       conn.Close()                    <- net.Dial
     17  os        defer f.Close()
      5  os/exec   return cmd.Run()
      2  io        stdinPipe.Close()               <- Cmd.StdinPipe

Since the io counts do not move while 317 sites gain a real module, the
classifier was ALREADY matching these by short name. So the gain is PRECISION,
not recall: a match that rested on the name `Close` matching some catalogued
`Close` now rests on the receiver's actual package. The recall claim in the
filing is not supported by this cohort and is not repeated here.

WHERE THE ITEM'S OWN INERT ROWS LIVE. Its discussion measured seven go framework
rows as inert — gin.Engine.Run, fiber.App.Listen, echo.Echo.Start and the rest —
every one entered through `gin.Default()` / `fiber.New()` / `echo.New()`. Those
are THIRD-PARTY, and ADR-0047 puts third-party rows in community overlays rather
than the shipped catalogue, so this half cannot move them by construction. The
overlay is a separate follow-up, not folded in here.

